  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nUpper ridge dominates the Central/Southern Plains into much of the \nsoutheastern US, while an amplified trough is off the Pacific coast \nand another upper low tracks across Ontario. A more subtle \nperturbation is noted off the High Plains, leading to a weak area of \nlow pressure in NE/IA. Tropical Storm Edouard is noted off the coast \nof TX/LA. The southwest flow plus the ample sunshine under the \npersistent ridge is leading to another afternoon with temperatures \nin the upper 90s up to 100 degrees with heat indices a couple of \ndegrees higher. A persistence forecast should do fairly well into \nWednesday, although we look to see a bit of an increase in dew \npoints as the tropical system comes further inland and the southerly \nflow brings that moisture northward. This should especially be the \ncase Thursday and Friday. As such, felt that the 50th percentile of \ndew points would be more representative than the drier solutions \npresented in the higher resolution models. Similar temperatures with \nslightly higher dew points would push us closer to heat indices \naround 105. Confidence in reaching that criteria is highest Thursday \nand Friday, and may not be quite as widespread Wednesday. Still \nopted to join northern and eastern neighbors in a Heat Advisory \nstarting Wednesday given impacts to outdoor activities and risk for \nheat stress and illnesses even with borderline conditions.\n\nBeyond Friday, the upper ridge looks to retrograde to the west with \nits center right over the forecast area through the Labor Day \nweekend. Long-range models start to show some diverging in the \nsolutions, resulting in some variation in how far south the upper \nridge will get pushed. With that in mind, high temperatures look to \nremain in the 90s through the middle of next week. Ensembles show a \nsmall range of only a few degrees through the weekend, and a need \nfor additional headlines beyond Friday will need to be considered \ngiven the favorable pattern for heat to continue. The range widens \nslightly into the middle of the week with the varying solutions.",
